        {
          "number": 105,
          "title": "Deeply nested config keys are ignored",
          "state": "open",
          "user": {
            "login": "cfgy-cat",
            "type": "User"
          },
          "labels": [
            {
              "name": "good first issue"
            },
            {
              "name": "config"
            }
          ],
          "comments": 1,
          "created_at": "2026-10-12T10:00:00Z",
          "updated_at": "2026-10-18T10:00:00Z",
          "html_url": "https://github.com/acme/widgets/issues/105",
          "body": "When a config key is nested more than three levels deep it silently vanishes after a round trip through the loader. I first noticed this while migrating a very large configuration file from our previous tool, and after some hours bisecting I narrowed it down to the merge step rather than the parse step, but I have not had time to dig further. The confusing part is that shallow keys survive fine, and the failure only shows up on write, so reads look normal until you reload the file. I tried a few workarounds with dotted aliases but those broke other things in ways that were hard to explain to my teammates, and eventually we agreed to keep the configuration shallow for now. The right fix probably belongs somewhere around the flattening logic, but a maintainer should confirm that before anyone starts moving code around. Reproduction requires a config with at least four nested sections and a reload cycle."
        },
